Here are some Machine Learning algorithms that can be implemented in Java to improve decision-making processes:
1. Decision Trees: Decision Trees are used for classification and regression tasks. It is a tree-like model where each internal node represents a test on an attribute, each branch represents the outcome of the test, and each leaf node represents a class label or a numerical value.
Here is a code snippet to demonstrate the usage of Decision Trees in Java:
<pre>
// Load dataset
Dataset dataset = new Dataset();
dataset.loadFromCSV("dataset.csv");
// Train model
DecisionTree decisionTree = new DecisionTree();
decisionTree.train(dataset);
// Make prediction
Instance instance = new Instance();
instance.setFeature("feature1", 0.6);
instance.setFeature("feature2", 0.4);
String predictedLabel = decisionTree.predict(instance);
// Print result
System.out.println("Predicted label: " + predictedLabel);
</pre>
2. Naive Bayes: Naive Bayes is a probabilistic algorithm that is used for classification tasks. It is based on Bayes' theorem, which states that the probability of a hypothesis (in this case, a class label) is proportional to the probability of the evidence (in this case, the features).
Here is a code snippet to demonstrate the usage of Naive Bayes in Java:
<pre>
// Load dataset
Dataset dataset = new Dataset();
dataset.loadFromCSV("dataset.csv");
// Train model
NaiveBayes naiveBayes = new NaiveBayes();
naiveBayes.train(dataset);
// Make prediction
